      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wearing Hanfu</h2>
      <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed">Hanfu has multiple layers, and the order matters. The shop assistant will help you put on the inner garment (zhongyi) first — a lightweight cotton or silk layer worn against the skin. Next comes the main robe (usually a cross-collar ao or a round-collar yuanlingpao), followed by the outer layer or coat. The sash or belt is tied at the waist, and hairstyling completes the look. For women, the process takes about 20-30 minutes; for men, about 15 minutes. Expect to spend 30-45 minutes total from selection to finished styling. Pro tip: wear a thin, neutral-colored top underneath so the inner garment sits smoothly, and avoid bulky clothing that shows through the fabric.</p>
      <div class="mt-4 bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-800 mb-1">⚠️ Common Hanfu Mistakes</p>
        <ul class="text-amber-700 space-y-1 ml-0 list-disc pl-5">
          <li><strong>Wrong collar direction:</strong> The collar always crosses right-over-left (右衽 yòurèn). Left-over-right (左衽) was historically reserved for burial garments. Rental shops check this, but if you're buying your own, remember: right side goes on top.</li>
          <li><strong>Sleeves dragging on the ground:</strong> Hanfu sleeves are long by design — they're meant to drape past the hands. When eating or using your phone, hold the sleeve with the opposite hand (a graceful, historically accurate gesture). Don't push sleeves up like a hoodie — it breaks the silhouette.</li>
          <li><strong>Choosing the wrong shoe:</strong> Sneakers under a flowing Ming dynasty aoqun ruin the effect. Most rental shops provide cloth shoes (布鞋 bùxié). If you're tall, flat shoes are fine — the robe covers them anyway.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-warm-800 mt-8 mb-3">Where to Rent Hanfu (City-by-City Guide)</h3>
